  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records._grounding_kernel. Its documented rule is: \"The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.\" The harness's own _grounding_verdict, which this function reads, returns SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT when the span is not in the document, and the function's code calls _span_occurs with no relaxations, whose documented rule is \"Return None when the span is not in the document, else how it was matched (\"verbatim\" or a relaxation).\" The two reply texts are: Reply A is the string '{\"value\": \"a b\", \"span\": \"a  b\", \"document\": \"a b\"}' and Reply B is the string '{\"value\": \"a b\", \"span\": \"a b\", \"document\": \"a b\"}'. For Reply A, the span \"a  b\" (two spaces) is not present verbatim in the document \"a b\" (one space), so the rule requires SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT. For Reply B, the span \"a b\" is present verbatim, and the value \"a b\" is in the span, so the rule requires GROUNDED. The check, however, normalizes whitespace in _span_occurs before checking, so it finds the normalized span \"a b\" in the normalized document \"a b\" for both replies, and then finds the value in the span, returning GROUNDED for both. Thus the check gives the same answer where the rule requires different answers. I would be wrong if the check returns SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT for Reply A and GROUNDED for Reply B, or if the rule as written is interpreted to allow whitespace normalization in the span-in-document check.",
